<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements.

  `com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit` is requested and is the ONLY entitlement here.
  The justification, written down so a future reader can re-argue it rather than guess:

    Arivu maps a ~396 MB GGUF and runs it. The weights are clean, file-backed pages, so jetsam
    charges very little of them to phys_footprint; what IS charged is the KV cache (~115 MB at 2048
    tokens, q8_0), the compute buffer (~28 MiB measured, D-028) and the app itself. That fits the
    ordinary limit on a 4 GB iPhone with modest headroom — so this entitlement is insurance against
    a transient spike during the first map, not a load-bearing part of the plan
    (leaves/MULTIPLATFORM.md, caution 1).

  If a future profile ships a larger model, this entitlement becomes load-bearing and must be
  VERIFIED on a 4 GB device before that profile is committed to. Apple grants it without review for
  most apps, but "we assumed it worked" is how a 2.5 GB model plan dies at submission.

  `com.apple.developer.kernel.extended-virtual-addressing` is deliberately NOT requested: it matters
  for mapping files larger than the 64-bit address-space cap a 32-bit-derived process gets, which a
  400 MB file is nowhere near. Requesting entitlements we do not need is the opposite of C3's claim.

  Nothing else. No keychain group, no App Groups, no push, no Siri, no associated domains.

  UNVERIFIED: not built, not signed, not submitted.

  spine: C2, C3
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
